azgor wrote:
> [1]: a blank scroll (or spellbook) dipped into !oGA becomes a random
> scroll (or spellbook) (in other words, it gains an ability/effect) with
> cursedness decided by the same rule as for the magic marker --azgor

Hmmm... I don't know.

> [2]: Quest leaders notice a PC's change of sex if the PC wears an
> amulet of changing before doing the quest. --aristotle73

Interesting -- not only logical, but it'd also be a hint regarding the
behavior of Helms of Opposite Alignment.

> [3]: Cavemen, barbarians, and orcs should be immune from "An urge to
> take a bath overwhelms you" when dealing with fountains. --PsyMar

Heh, funny idea.  Doesn't really affect the game much, though.

> [4]: Enough wisdom (>20) should occasionally act as intrinsic food
> appraisal --Tapin

I really like the idea of giving the attributes a greater role in play,
they've kind of become undervalued as Nethack's grown.

> [5]: Zapping fire at a corpse should cook the corpse, making it rot
> slower.  Do this too much and you'll burn the meal, causing it to grant
> almost no nutrition. --Grunt

One could argue whether Nethack needs food *cooking* or not.  It's an
interesting idea if it lends some important game-enhancing element.

> [6]: (5 continued) Add marshmallows and allow one to apply it to a
> spear which can then be applied over a flaming corpse. --aristotle73

Not fond of this one.

> [7]: (6 continued) Of course, you would have to be able to cook them
> over lava as well.  Or extremely slowly over lamps, lanterns, candles,
> etc. --Grunt

Not too interesting.

> [8]: (7 continued) Applying the masrshmallow to a lembas wafer and a
> candybar makes smores. --aristotle73

Sigh.

> [9]: Getting hit by a paper golem yields the YAFM "The paper golem
> hits.  You are cut!  OUCH!  The paper cut is so small but it hurts SO
> MUCH!" --aristotle73

YAFMs are best when they don't show up every game.  Most players
encounter at least one paper golem in a game, so this would probably
get overexposed.

> [10]: jerky maker: Jerky is non-perishable unless it becomes wet, in
> which case it reverts to a corpse. It has 100% the nutrition of the
> original corpse but only half the weight. Cockatrice jerky has a 100%
> chance of stiffening an unprotected player and a 1d20 chance of stoning
> a struck monster. The age of the corpse is not reset when the jerky is
> rehydrated. --azgor

Aren't tins and tinning kits enough?

> [11]: NetHack should disallow greasing of gloves and footwear if it
> doesn't already. "The grease seems unable to coat the foo."
> --aristotle73

Why not?

> [12]: R - a mysterious force beast. "You hit the mysterious force
> beast. Your sword is deflected by a mysterious force! You are struck by
> a mysterious force!" --GreyKnight

So, these would be invisible?  Anyway, bad jokes do not necessarily
make good game features.

> [13]: 0 - a katamari ball. (a container) "You roll the katamari ball.
> The orc is killed! An orc corpse sticks to the katamari ball. An orcish
> dagger sticks to the katamari ball." --GreyKnight

But katamaris don't kill anyone!  Anyway, again, jokes (especially
in-jokes) don't necessarily make good gameplay.

> [14]: When #chatting with a cancelled succubus: "Let's just be
> friends!" (the succubus remains hostile) --lament

It already gives the "severe headache" message.

> [15]: It should go both ways. When #chatting with a cancelled incubus:
> "Call me up sometime and we can get dinner!" If you try to (C)all the
